---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Six queues and a reserved worker — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="The background decode system in LightPhotos, told as the sequence of problems that produced it. None of the problems are photo-specific."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-08-20">August 20, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Six queues and a reserved worker</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          The background decode system in LightPhotos, told as the sequence of
          problems that produced it. Six queues and a worker that refuses the
          most common job sounds like over-engineering. Every piece is there
          because something visibly broke without it.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 5 min read</p>
      </header>

      <h2>One queue, N workers</h2>
      <p>
        LightPhotos decodes images on a pool of background threads so the
        interface stays responsive. My first version was the obvious thing. One
        queue, a handful of workers, first in first out.
      </p>
      <p>
        It works right up until you scroll. Scrolling the grid queues a hundred
        thumbnail decodes. Then you double-click a photo, and your photo goes on
        the end of the queue behind a hundred thumbnails you are no longer
        looking at. The app looks frozen while it diligently finishes work for a
        part of the screen you already scrolled past.
      </p>

      <h2>Not all jobs are equal</h2>
      <p>
        So I split the one queue into six, ordered by how much you care about
        the result right now. The photo you just opened jumps ahead of the
        thumbnails behind it.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 240" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: six priority queues drain in order into a pool of eight decode workers, one of which is reserved and never accepts thumbnail jobs.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="24">SIX QUEUES, DRAINED IN THIS ORDER</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="36" width="300" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="53">1</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="30" y="53">First pass of the photo you opened</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="68" width="300" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="85">2</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="30" y="85">Sharp second pass of that photo</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="100" width="300" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="117">3</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="30" y="117">Full-resolution decode, on demand</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="132" width="300" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="149">4</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="30" y="149">Camera and lens metadata</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="164" width="300" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="181">5</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="30" y="181">Thumbnails for the grid</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="196" width="300" height="26" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="12" y="213">6</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="30" y="213">Capture times for burst grouping</text>

          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="310" y1="96" x2="368" y2="96"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M368 90 L368 102 L380 96 Z"></path>

          <path class="lp-fig-line-soft" d="M300 177 H340 V221 H374" stroke-dasharray="3 4"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" d="M332 191 L348 207 M348 191 L332 207"></path>

          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="380" y="24">8 DECODE WORKERS</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="380" y="36" width="240" height="20" rx="4"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="380" y="61" width="240" height="20" rx="4"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="380" y="86" width="240" height="20" rx="4"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="380" y="111" width="240" height="20" rx="4"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="380" y="136" width="240" height="20" rx="4"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="380" y="161" width="240" height="20" rx="4"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="380" y="186" width="240" height="20" rx="4"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="380" y="211" width="240" height="20" rx="4"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="392" y="226">reserved &middot; never takes thumbnails</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The ordering that surprises people is metadata beating thumbnails. The
          metadata panel sits next to a photo you are already looking at, so a
          delay there is obvious, while a thumbnail two rows down is not yet on
          screen.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <blockquote class="lp-pullquote">
        Priority is about what you are currently staring at, not about what is
        cheap.
      </blockquote>

      <h2>Priority only controls who gets in</h2>
      <p>
        With six queues in place, the freeze got shorter and did not go away.
        Priority governs the order in which queued work is <em>started</em>. It
        says nothing about work already running. If all eight workers picked up
        thumbnails one millisecond before you double-clicked, your photo is at
        the front of the queue and nobody is free to take it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The textbook answer is preemption. Cancel a running decode and hand the
        worker over. That was not available to me, because the decode happens
        inside somebody else's library that has no idea I want it to stop.
      </p>
      <p>
        What was available is reservation. One worker never accepts thumbnails
        at all. It sits idle through a flood of thumbnail work and is there the
        moment you open a photo. On a single-core machine I disable the
        reservation, because reserving the only worker starves the grid.
      </p>

      <h2>A worker that can say no breaks the wake-up</h2>
      <p>
        Workers sleep when the queue is empty, and adding a job wakes one up.
        That is the standard pattern, and it is correct as long as every
        sleeping worker is interchangeable. My reserved worker is not. It can
        decline.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 190" role="img" aria-label="Sequence: a queued thumbnail wakes one worker, which happens to be the reserved worker; it declines the job and goes back to sleep while the other seven workers are never woken.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">WAKING ONE SLEEPER, WHEN ONE SLEEPER CAN SAY NO</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="32" width="140" height="48" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="70" y="52" text-anchor="middle">Thumbnail</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="70" y="68" text-anchor="middle">queued</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M146 50 L146 62 L158 56 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="170" y="32" width="140" height="48" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="240" y="52" text-anchor="middle">Wake one</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="240" y="68" text-anchor="middle">worker</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M316 50 L316 62 L328 56 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="340" y="32" width="220" height="48" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="450" y="52" text-anchor="middle">The reserved worker looks,</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="450" y="68" text-anchor="middle">declines, goes back to sleep</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="340" y="104" width="22" height="22" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="371" y="104" width="22" height="22" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="402" y="104" width="22" height="22" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="433" y="104" width="22" height="22" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="464" y="104" width="22" height="22" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="495" y="104" width="22" height="22" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="526" y="104" width="22" height="22" rx="3"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="340" y="148">the other seven were never woken</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="0" y="112">No error. No crash.</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="132">Just work that sits still, until</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="152">something unrelated wakes somebody.</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The fix is to wake all of them instead of just one.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The lesson is bigger than the fix. Waking one consumer assumes every
        consumer can handle every item. The moment you let a worker refuse a
        job, that assumption is gone, and what you get is not a crash. It is
        work that mysteriously sits still.
      </p>

      <h2>Marking work as started when nobody started it</h2>
      <p>
        The loader keeps a set of "already asked for this" markers so it does
        not queue the same photo five times, and clears each one when the job
        finishes. In the browser build the pool starts no threads at all, so
        nothing was ever going to finish them.
      </p>
      <p>
        The symptom was not an error. It was the app waking sixty times a second
        for the rest of the session, on a laptop battery, to check on work that
        did not exist.
      </p>
      <p>
        My fix was to treat the marker as a lease. Queueing now returns one of
        three answers, which are accepted, no workers exist, or the pool is
        broken. The caller records the job as in flight only on the first. Any
        loading flag, any in-flight set, any deduplication key is a lease
        somebody has to release. Set it only after the party responsible for
        releasing it has agreed to take the work.
      </p>

      <h2>In a browser, workers die</h2>
      <p>
        Porting the pool to a browser tab turned it into something closer to a
        process supervisor, because a fresh Web Worker drops any message sent
        before its script first yields. Each worker now announces itself when it
        is ready, and one that stays silent for ten seconds is treated as
        failed.
      </p>
      <p>
        A failed slot gets replaced up to three times, then marked permanently
        unavailable. That is deliberately different from "not ready yet", and
        the distinction fixes a real deadlock. Export work waits while running
        jobs are at or above capacity, and capacity counts ready workers.
        Without a terminal state, a slot stuck mid-replacement reads as not
        ready, capacity reads zero, and the progress bar waits forever.
      </p>

      <h2>The part that made all of this testable</h2>
      <p>
        None of the above needs threads to test, because the scheduling rules
        live in a plain data structure. A test builds one, pushes a job of each
        kind in reverse priority order, drains it and checks the order. Another
        drains it as the reserved worker and checks that thumbnails get skipped.
      </p>
      <p>
        Concurrent systems usually have a deterministic core, which is the
        policy about who runs what and in what order, wrapped in a
        non-deterministic shell. Pull the core out and you get to test the part
        that encodes your decisions, in microseconds, without a single flaky
        run.
      </p>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Priority decides who gets in, reservation decides who gets
          through.</strong> If urgent work can be blocked by background work
          that already started, no amount of queue reordering helps.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reserve capacity before you build cancellation.</strong>
          Keeping one worker free is a few lines. Interrupting a decode inside
          somebody else's library is usually impossible.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A worker that can refuse a job breaks the textbook
          pool.</strong> Once one consumer can decline, the failure is silence,
          not an error.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Every "this is being handled" flag needs one owner who clears
          it.</strong> List every way that owner can fail to exist, and have the
          enqueue call return a value that tells them apart.
        </li>
      </ul>




